#
# The user the board's file and time host runs as.
#
# ozd refuses to run as root, in its own words, and it is right to: nothing it
# does needs a privilege, and as root a path check it got wrong would reach the
# whole filesystem rather than one directory.  This board otherwise runs
# everything as root, so the user has to be made, and Buildroot's users table
# is the way to make one.
#
# The fields are Buildroot's own: user, uid, group, gid, password, home, shell,
# groups, comment.  A uid of -1 lets Buildroot choose one.  The password is
# locked and the shell is /bin/false, because nobody logs in as this user: the
# init script starts the program under it with start-stop-daemon's -c.
#
# The home directory is what the program's base root sits under, and that root
# is where a Lisp Machine user's own directory goes.  It is in the root
# filesystem, which is a RAM disk unpacked at every boot, so it is empty at
# every boot and costs the card nothing.
ozd -1 ozd -1 * /var/lib/ozd /bin/false - the board's file and time host
